0 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124770 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- **II.** $Pq=q$\n- **III.** $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124770 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3662430d-84f0-5632-b066-9a7b273fafeb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124770 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1.4}{15}+\\frac{5.6}{15} \\\\ \\frac{5.6}{15}+\\frac{2.4}{15}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{bmatrix}=q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector such that $Pq=q.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124771 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460567 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186124771 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460568 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186124771 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460569 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186124771 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124771 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124771 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- **II.** $Pq=q$\n- **III.** $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124771 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5d2ea1fa-42c3-5215-bede-8b745015a967" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124771 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0+\\frac{1.5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1.5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ \\frac{5}{8}\\end{bmatrix}\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124772 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124772 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110242" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124772 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124772 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124772 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "496d0736-5684-529d-8dd0-8b061c9a6089" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124772 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{6}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{6}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{1}{7}-\\frac{6}{7}=-\\frac{5}{7}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124774 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124774 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124774 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111172" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124774 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124774 #edb/ref 1071 "The migration table below describes the movement of the population between cities and suburbs every year in a certain state. \n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Cities** | **Suburbs** |\n| To: | **Cities** | $0.8$ | $0.7$ |\n| **Suburbs** | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |  |\n\nThat is, $20%$ of those living in cities move to the suburbs each year, and $70%$ of those living in suburbs move to the cities.\n\nAssuming that the population of the state is $9$ million and remains constant, how many people would eventually live in cities if the given probabilities remain constant over many years?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124774 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "57c92fa7-df07-5594-a6b7-bd705bfe80a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124774 #edb/ref 1074 "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{7}{9}$ of the $9$ million population will live in cities. Therefore, the total number of people living in cities after many years will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{9}\\cdot 9\\,\\text{million}=7\\,\\text{million}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125565 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125565 #edb/ref 1061 34230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125565 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "965fe16f-9c57-5058-9e32-90332314977c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125565 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125566 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125566 #edb/ref 1061 34231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125566 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bd127c03-d025-5b21-ae93-e24b7aec916f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125566 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125567 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125567 #edb/ref 1061 34232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125567 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "429ea8df-33bc-5c79-a0d6-97b71166cd98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125567 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125568 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125568 #edb/ref 1061 34233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125568 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ba24a30-9b4e-5e58-93b1-b999bb45fe16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125568 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125569 #edb/ref 1061 34234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125569 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3865f17c-09ee-5b73-96f9-45f1dd985dd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125569 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125570 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125570 #edb/ref 1061 34235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125570 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "735b7b96-43fa-5c0e-b4b8-df7598a6610b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125570 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125588 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125588 #edb/ref 1061 37332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125588 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7c429a3d-9dd8-5fe2-a7e7-cf0e9f515d1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125588 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125849 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a stochastic matrix $P$ and a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}.$ Then, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a **steady-state vector** of $P$ if \n\n$$\nP\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nFor example, to show that the stochastic vector \n![](images/ca/caba3c1b2f3245a3ebea0bd6ff6d47c63d409a7ba18a48db1f1269cb9ac13854.svg) \n\nis a steady-state vector for the stochastic matrix \n![](images/7f/7f231d09e7daf85eec90e682a9eb53c242aadac75b372e3685b65757ae3309dc.svg) \n\nwe compute the matrix product $P\\mathbf q\\mathbin:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =[\\begin{aligned}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.94+2\\cdot 0.03 \\\\ 0.06+2\\cdot 0.97\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =q\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $P\\mathbf q = \\mathbf q,$ the vector $\\mathbf q$ is a steady-state vector of $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125849 #edb/ref 1065 5500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125849 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e1601d67-32bc-5796-a800-6e704f25f991" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125849 #edb/ref 1067 "Steady-State Vectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125850 #edb/ref 1064 "To find a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P,$ we seek a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that \n\n$$\nP\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nLet's begin by writing this equation in a slightly different form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector we seek is a solution to the matrix equation $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's use this idea to find a steady-state vector for the matrix\n![](images/77/77ffe4078d3c62c30ab94b59bc9465a92db659b9e70b137bb144deb44844e800.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute $(P-I):$\n![](images/3d/3d91ece6103baa469569adf1994f3965cfc3429fbe65c0d7cfffff53b83b20ef.svg)\n\nThen, to solve $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0},$ we reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.75 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0.75 & -0.5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.75 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\nx_1= \\dfrac 23 x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/4a/4a5d81320f3e4eeee6b9fb09424238467d6c9e8b2e2246971ba3550b65a6d9c2.svg)\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, our steady-state vector is\n\n![](images/85/8515cb27fa64a5186fae0c63184df2ff7651c03963eef5804c4d6bedbace2283.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125850 #edb/ref 1065 5501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125850 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "78f635ff-e2e7-5c9f-9ec2-470187a45bc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125850 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125851 #edb/ref 1064 "A stochastic matrix $P$ is **regular** if there exists a positive integer $n$ such that $P^n$ has all positive elements.\n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- The stochastic matrix $P,$ given by\n![](images/e9/e9588bb9c77762d6caee5460403077eba65a891676f85a9e30ff99e7e6112184.svg) \nhas all positive elements. Therefore, it is a regular stochastic matrix.\n\n- The stochastic matrix $Q,$ given by\n![](images/e0/e0ba33618820d19e77d365a9f316239ef2e36133cbcac4007bf2b5c2f6e57137.svg) \ncontains a zero element. However, if we compute its square, we get\n![](images/29/29c5a411ac3c797699a39e35972e415e98df3347e66581bb5494f61a92d92bd5.svg) \nwhich has all positive elements. Therefore, $Q$ is a regular stochastic matrix.\n\nRegular stochastic matrices have the following important property:\n\n*Any regular stochastic matrix has a **unique** steady-state vector $\\mathbf q.$ Moreover, the Markov chain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}, \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮ \\\\ {x}_{k+1} & =P{x}_{k} \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nconverges to $\\mathbf q$ for **any** initial state $\\mathbf x_0.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125851 #edb/ref 1065 5502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125851 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "255fadf0-9fc2-52d5-976b-015a97f52b08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125851 #edb/ref 1067 "Regular Stochastic Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125855 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a population where people live either in the city or the countryside. A study carried out by the local authority shows that, each year,\n\n- $6\\%$ of the city population relocates to the countryside,\n\n- $3\\%$ of the countryside population relocates to the city, and\n\n- everyone else remains where they are.\n\nAdditionally, $70\\%$ of the population lived in the city and $30\\%$ in the countryside at the time of the study. \n\nWe can represent this situation using a stochastic matrix $P$ and initial state vector $\\mathbf x_0,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/44/44fa9ad32622bd1d7b3541206030ecc862a3db9420f46049c9f16992fdb947ca.svg) \n\nAs we've seen, to find the state vector $\\mathbf x_{k+1}$ that corresponds to the $(k+1)$th year, we left-multiply the state vector corresponding to the $k$th year by $P.$ This gives rise to the following Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}, \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮ \\\\ {x}_{k+1} & =P{x}_{k} \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe local authority wants to determine whether the state vectors settle down to a fixed state. With the aid of a computer, they compute the following states:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.667 \\\\ 0.333\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0.636\\,97 \\\\ 0.363\\,03\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0.609\\,64 \\\\ 0.390\\,36\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{100} & =P{x}_{99}=[\\begin{aligned}0.333\\,363 \\\\ 0.666\\,637\\end{aligned}]≈[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{101} & =P{x}_{100}=[\\begin{aligned}0.333\\,36 \\\\ 0.666\\,64\\end{aligned}]≈[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the data, we see that the vectors $\\mathbf x_{100}$ and $\\mathbf x_{101}$ are both approximately equal to the vector\n\n![](images/03/030d5719a268d9e15c2c58488ff1c36602d5c757475e79c25dcd1da5a4eb8712.svg)\n\nFurthermore, computing $\\mathbf x_{102}, \\mathbf{x}_{103}, \\ldots$ all give vectors approximately equal to $\\mathbf q.$ This suggests that the sequence $\\mathbf x_k$ converges to $\\mathbf q\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx_k \\rightarrow \\mathbf q \\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad k\\to\\infty.\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf q$ is called a **steady-state vector** of the matrix $P.$ Here, our steady-state vector suggests that after enough time has passed, one-third of the population will live in the city and two-thirds will live in the countryside.\n\nFinally, notice that for large values of $k,$ we have\n\n$$\nP\\mathbf x_k \\approx \\mathbf x_{k}.\n$$\n\nThis observation indicates how to find $\\mathbf q$ without resorting to computers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125855 #edb/ref 1065 6151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125855 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cb9435c4-44ed-58ec-9c04-b8fed4403fcd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125855 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244636 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{5}{6}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244636 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244636 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "245874df-8c61-5865-89e0-2eea58d0ddb2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245524 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{5}{7}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245524 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245524 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "89ef273f-1ef4-5830-8401-02da27fbfef8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245561 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{7}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245561 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245561 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8f0491b5-2aa5-5777-aa10-2469c7c918ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245909 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{2}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245909 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245909 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b33e12af-527b-59a1-a8b0-87c0b7d8c14d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246248 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246248 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246248 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d997fab9-f7e0-5e91-9d3c-7082d52b45a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246791 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186244636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759