edb/ref 17592186268300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268744 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186268392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268744 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268744 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268744 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186268300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268744 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bcddcb02-38f8-5b6f-bc4a-2daf7cc42e53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269025 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186464949 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186269025 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186464950 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186269025 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186464951 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186269025 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135307" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269025 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269025 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- **II.** $Pq=q$\n- **III.** $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269025 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56012d7d-0dc5-5803-80a1-167efecb090a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269025 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.02+0.24 \\\\ 0.18+0.56\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.26 \\\\ 0.74\\end{bmatrix}\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269032 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269032 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135325" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269032 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269032 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.3 \\\\ 1 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269032 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e24f728-ce2c-55b9-a28e-e90dcc5c4465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269032 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.3 \\\\ 1 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0.3 \\\\ 1 & -0.3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -0.3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+0.3{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 10{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{10}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{10}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{10}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{13}{10}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{10}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{13} \\\\ \\frac{10}{13}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{3}{13}-\\frac{10}{13}=-\\frac{7}{13}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269035 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269035 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269035 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269035 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.6 \\\\ 1 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269035 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "69190782-47c3-5351-a63c-037e5f837593" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269035 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.6 \\\\ 1 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0.6 \\\\ 1 & -0.6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -0.6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+0.6{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{8}{5}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ \\frac{5}{8}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{3}{8}-\\frac{5}{8}=-\\frac{1}{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269036 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269036 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135344" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269036 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269036 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.2 & 0.65 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & 0.25\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a+b-c?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269036 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "26bfb2a9-4e8e-55d5-877c-2b96948f29b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269036 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.2 & 0.65 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & 0.25\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.3 & -0.8 & 0.65 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & -0.75\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.2 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.3 & -0.8 & 0.65 & 0 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & -0.75 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=5{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -8 & 6.5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -7.5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}+0.5{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b-c=\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269039 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269039 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135352" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269039 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269039 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a+b-c?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269039 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "523b5c51-5d69-5337-be0d-a6c9d40f6678" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269039 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & -0.6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.4 & 0.4 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & -0.6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10 & -14 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{14}{10}{x}_{3}=\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+2(\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{3})-6{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{3}+\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{20}{5}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{7}{20} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b-c=\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{7}{20}-\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{10}{20}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269056 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269056 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135408" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269056 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269056 #edb/ref 1071 "There are two groups of people In a small town: those who travel to work by car and those who travel by train. Every year a study is done in which people are asked about their preference between using trains versus cars. The table below shows the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Car** | **Train** |\n| To: | **Car** | $0.35$ | $0.25$ |\n| **Train** | $0.65$ | $0.75$ |  |\n\nIt's found that $65%$ of people who currently travel by car will switch to train next year, while $25%$ of people who currently travel by train will switch to their car next year.\n\nAssuming that the town has $90\\,000$ people that travel to work by train or car, and the given probabilities remain constant over many years, how many people will eventually use the train to travel to work?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269056 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2ead374-20fc-5336-8f26-ef768edbfcce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269056 #edb/ref 1074 "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.35 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.75\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.35 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.75\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.65 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.65 & -0.25\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.65 & 0.25 & 0 \\\\ 0.65 & -0.25 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.65 & 0.25 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{18}{13}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{13}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ \\frac{13}{18}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{13}{18}$ of the people will eventually use the train to travel. Therefore, the number of people who will eventually use the train is\n\n$$\n\\frac{13}{18}\\cdot 90\\,000=65\\,000.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269453 #edb/ref 1080 "180" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269453 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269453 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "157f5ca9-b81c-53c8-b1d3-29be34acd7e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269552 #edb/ref 1080 "540" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269552 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269552 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "21680cff-4228-5c00-9484-24e5b719268c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269768 #edb/ref 1080 "720" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269768 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269768 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3728440d-e1a2-5207-95ac-9416bd057cf4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270700 #edb/ref 1080 "630" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270700 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270700 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a163dbe7-d913-56ca-a615-fbf7e9977ea8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271123 #edb/ref 1080 "360" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271123 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271123 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d28a917b-3f74-5562-b106-c78d82823886" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271579 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271579 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271579 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271579 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271579 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186271123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271579 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271579 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271579 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186271123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271579 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "264e87e8-2ef0-5cf0-82c6-75d0173c6ac2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272188 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272188 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141035" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272188 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272188 #edb/ref 1071 "A study shows how the sports preferences of young people in a certain community vary from year to year. The following table shows the results of this study. \n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Baseball** | **Basketball** | **Football** |\n| To: | **Baseball** | $0.8$ | $0.2$ | $0$ |\n| **Basketball** | $0.1$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ |  |\n| **Football** | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |  |\n\nFor example, $10%$ of people who used to prefer baseball now prefer football. \n\nGiven that $1\\,260$ young people were interviewed for the study and that the probabilities listed remain constant for many years, how many of those people will eventually prefer baseball?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272188 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "03023a20-ec6d-5cf1-9ba8-6fa6f21acb01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272188 #edb/ref 1074 "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & 0.8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & 0.8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & -0.2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & -0.2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=-5{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}+0{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{3}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{2}{7}$ of the people will prefer baseball. Therefore, the number of people who will prefer baseball is\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot 1\\,260=360\\,\\text{people}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186388820 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186388821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388820 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141024" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388820 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388820 #edb/ref 1071 "A study conducted on $3\\,400$ teenagers by the transportation department of a particular city reveals that teenagers' preference for the type of home-to-school transportation changes with time. The following table shows the results of this study.  \n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Bus** | **Bicycle** | **Electric Scooter ** |\n| To: | **Bus** | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0$ |\n| **Bicycle** | $0.4$ | $0.4$ | $0.4$ |  |\n| **Electric Scooter** | $0.4$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ |  |\n\nFor example, $40%$ of teenagers who used to go to school by bus now go by bicycle.  \n\nHow many teenagers would eventually go to school on electric scooters if the listed probabilities remained constant over many years?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388820 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a031dbeb-978c-5542-b4e9-e4e60f2dc984" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388820 #edb/ref 1074 "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.8 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & -0.4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.8 & 0.2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.4 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & -0.4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=5{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=5{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3})-{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{4}{10} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{1}{2}$ of the teenagers will go to school in electric scooters. Therefore, the total number of teenagers in the city that will go to school in electric scooters will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\,400=1\\,700.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186388821 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388821 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388821 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388821 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388821 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388821 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388821 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388821 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186388826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388821 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c17d838d-64b9-5408-9e52-5f894639d0cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388822 #edb/ref 1080 "1\\,360" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388822 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388822 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "aea59a55-ca8c-5011-94bb-adc5e2783f76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388823 #edb/ref 1080 "340" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388823 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388823 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8e324f12-a685-504b-be59-f46dd3f2728f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388824 #edb/ref 1080 "680" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388824 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388824 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "02091894-fa17-5170-9a72-9c82ed0e6bbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
